sequenceDiagram
    %% @spec walkthrough/2
    %% @title Payment idempotency
    %% @trigger The user taps Pay on the checkout page. A double tap, a reload or a retry after a lost response re-enters the same flow with the same key.
    %% @term idempotency key: a unique id the client sends with a request so the server can recognise a repeat of that exact request and give the same answer without doing the work twice.
    %% @term idempotent: safe to repeat; doing it twice has the same result as doing it once.
    %% @term JWT: JSON Web Token, a signed string that says who the user is; the server checks the signature instead of looking the user up on every request.
    %% @term SET NX: a Redis write that only succeeds when the key does not exist yet, so exactly one request can claim it.
    %% @term body hash: a short fingerprint (SHA-256) of the request body, used to notice when the same key is reused with different data.
    %% @term replay: sending the saved answer of an earlier request again instead of running the request a second time.
    %% @term event bus: an in-process notice board; one part of the code announces "payment paid" and every listener reacts, without the payment code knowing them.
    %% @term job queue: a waiting line in Redis for work that can happen later; a worker process takes jobs from it one by one and retries failed ones.
    %% @layer U kind=person The person paying. Taps Pay, and sometimes taps it twice.
    %% @layer FE kind=client Checkout page in the browser. Creates the Idempotency-Key and keeps it until the payment settles.
    %% @layer APP kind=app Express app in src/app.ts. Mounts the payment router under /api/v1 and sends every response, including errors from its error handler.
    %% @layer RT kind=router src/routes/payment.routes.ts. Maps POST /payments to its middleware chain and to PaymentController.create.
    %% @layer MW kind=middleware src/middleware. Runs authenticate, validate and idempotency in that order; the idempotency middleware makes one key mean one charge.
    %% @layer VAL kind=validator src/validators/payment.validator.ts. The Zod schema that decides whether the body is acceptable.
    %% @layer CTRL kind=controller src/controllers/payment.controller.ts. Turns the HTTP request into a service call and the result into a 201.
    %% @layer SVC kind=service src/services/payment.service.ts. Creates the payment, charges the card and announces the result.
    %% @layer REPO kind=repository src/repositories/payment.repository.ts. The only code that writes SQL for payments.
    %% @layer DB kind=db Postgres. Holds the payments table.
    %% @layer SER kind=serializer src/serializers/payment.serializer.ts. Picks the fields the client may see.
    %% @layer RDS kind=cache Redis. Holds one idem:<key> entry per idempotency key for 24 hours.
    %% @layer EB kind=eventbus src/events/payment.events.ts. Node EventEmitter; its payment.paid listener queues the receipt email.
    %% @layer MQ kind=queue BullMQ queue "mail" in Redis. Keeps email jobs until the mail worker takes them.
    %% @layer MWK kind=worker src/workers/mail.worker.ts. Separate process that sends one email per job and retries on failure.
    %% @layer PSP kind=external External payment provider. Also deduplicates on the key we forward.
    %% @layer MAIL kind=external Transactional email provider.
    autonumber
    actor U as User
    participant FE as Web App
    participant APP as App
    participant RT as Routes
    participant MW as Middleware
    participant VAL as Validator
    participant CTRL as Controller
    participant SVC as Service
    participant REPO as Repository
    participant DB as Database
    participant SER as Serializer
    participant RDS as Redis
    participant EB as Event Bus
    participant MQ as Mail Queue
    participant MWK as Mail Worker
    participant PSP as Payment Provider
    participant MAIL as Email Provider

    %% @step The user presses Pay. Nothing has been sent yet, so this is the moment to decide which key this attempt uses.
    U->>FE: Click "Pay ₹1,499"
    %% @step The page looks for a key saved for this cart. Reusing it means a reload or double tap is treated as the same attempt, not a new charge.
    %% @payload.result sessionStorage["pay:cart_77"] = "4f9c2e1a-7b7d-4c55-9a51-3e0d6b2f8c10"
    %% @uses hook useIdempotencyKey src/checkout/useIdempotencyKey.ts:8 Reads the key for this cart from sessionStorage, or creates a new UUID and saves it.
    %% @ref src/checkout/pay.ts:31
    FE->>FE: Reuse key from sessionStorage<br/>or create a new UUID
    %% @step The key travels in a header so the server can recognise repeats of this exact request.
    %% @payload.headers Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOi…
    %% @payload.headers Idempotency-Key: 4f9c2e1a-7b7d-4c55-9a51-3e0d6b2f8c10
    %% @payload.body { "cartId": "cart_77", "amount": 149900, "currency": "INR" }
    %% @schema cartId string, required → 422 "cartId is required"
    %% @schema amount integer in minor units, greater than 0 → 422 "amount must be positive"
    %% @schema currency one of INR, USD, EUR → 422 "currency must be INR, USD or EUR"
    %% @idempotent Repeats with the same key get the first answer back.
    %% @timeout 10 s on the browser side, then the user may tap again with the same key.
    %% @ref src/checkout/pay.ts:42
    FE->>APP: POST /api/v1/payments<br/>Idempotency-Key: 4f9c…
    %% @step The app matches the /api/v1/payments prefix and hands the request to the payment router.
    %% @ref src/app.ts:24
    APP->>RT: /api/v1/payments → paymentRoutes
    %% @step The route lists three middlewares before the controller. They run in this order, and any of them can stop the request.
    %% @ref src/routes/payment.routes.ts:9
    RT->>MW: router.post("/", authenticate,<br/>validate(createPaymentSchema), idempotency)

    %% @section Middleware checks
    %% @summary Who is paying, and is the body valid? Each failure stops the request before any money moves.
    %% @step authenticate() checks the JWT signature and expiry, then puts the user on the request for the next layers.
    %% @payload.input Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOi…
    %% @payload.result req.user = { "id": "u_9", "email": "ana@example.com" }
    %% @uses env JWT_SECRET src/config/index.ts:8 The key the token signature is checked with.
    %% @ref src/middleware/auth.middleware.ts:12
    MW->>MW: authenticate()<br/>verify JWT → req.user = u_9
    break Token missing or invalid
        %% @step The token is missing, expired or forged, so the middleware stops and hands an error to the app's error handler.
        %% @ref src/middleware/auth.middleware.ts:18
        MW-->>APP: next(new UnauthorizedError())
        %% @step The browser gets a 401 and the user is asked to sign in again. Nothing was charged.
        %% @payload.body { "error": "unauthorized", "message": "Invalid or expired token" }
        %% @ref src/app.ts:41
        APP-->>FE: 401 Unauthorized<br/>"Invalid or expired token"
    end
    %% @step validate() hands the body to the payment schema before any business code sees it.
    %% @payload.input { "cartId": "cart_77", "amount": 149900, "currency": "INR" }
    %% @ref src/middleware/validate.middleware.ts:7
    MW->>VAL: createPaymentSchema.safeParse(req.body)
    %% @step The schema returns the cleaned body, which replaces req.body from here on.
    %% @payload.result { "success": true, "data": { "cartId": "cart_77", "amount": 149900, "currency": "INR" } }
    %% @ref src/validators/payment.validator.ts:4
    VAL-->>MW: success: cartId, amount, currency
    break Body invalid
        %% @step A field broke a schema rule, so the request stops here with the list of problems.
        %% @ref src/middleware/validate.middleware.ts:10
        MW-->>APP: next(new ValidationError(issues))
        %% @step The browser gets a 422 naming each wrong field. Nothing was claimed or charged.
        %% @payload.body { "error": "validation_failed", "details": [{ "field": "amount", "message": "amount must be positive" }] }
        %% @ref src/app.ts:41
        APP-->>FE: 422 Unprocessable Entity<br/>"amount must be positive"
    end

    %% @section Claim the key
    %% @summary Redis, not the server, decides which request owns the key.
    %% @step idempotency() hashes the body and tries to claim the key. SET NX lets exactly one request write it; every other request gets nil.
    %% @payload.input SET idem:4f9c… '{"status":"running","bodyHash":"sha256:5f…"}' NX EX 86400
    %% @state before no idem:4f9c… entry, or one from an earlier attempt
    %% @state after one entry with status running owned by this request, or nothing changed
    %% @uses util hashBody src/utils/hash.ts:5 SHA-256 of the raw request body, so a reused key with a different body can be noticed.
    %% @uses env IDEMPOTENCY_TTL_SECONDS src/config/index.ts:14 How long a key is kept (86400 by default).
    %% @ref src/middleware/idempotency.middleware.ts:21
    MW->>RDS: SET idem:4f9c… NX EX 86400
    %% @step OK means this request owns the key. nil means the key was used before.
    RDS-->>MW: OK (claimed) or nil (exists)
    Note over MW,RDS: SET NX is the lock
    break Key already used (nil)
        %% @section Repeats
        %% @summary A repeat never reaches the controller: it gets the saved answer, a wait, or a refusal.
        %% @step The key exists, so the middleware reads what happened to the first request with it.
        %% @ref src/middleware/idempotency.middleware.ts:27
        MW->>RDS: GET idem:4f9c…
        %% @step The saved entry for this key comes back.
        %% @payload.result { "status": "done", "bodyHash": "sha256:5f…", "code": 201, "body": { "id": "pay_456", "status": "paid" } }
        RDS-->>MW: status, bodyHash, code, body
        alt Done, same body (the response was lost)
            %% @step The saved answer is sent again, marked as a replay. The controller is not called.
            %% @ref src/middleware/idempotency.middleware.ts:33
            MW-->>APP: res.status(201).json(saved body)<br/>Idempotent-Replayed: true
            %% @step The browser receives the original answer again. No second charge happened.
            %% @payload.headers Idempotent-Replayed: true
            %% @payload.body { "id": "pay_456", "status": "paid", "amount": 149900, "currency": "INR" }
            %% @ref src/middleware/idempotency.middleware.ts:33
            APP-->>FE: 201 Created<br/>Idempotent-Replayed: true
        else Still running
            %% @step The first request is still being processed, so this one is refused instead of charging again.
            %% @ref src/middleware/idempotency.middleware.ts:37
            MW-->>APP: next(new ConflictError("request_in_progress"))
            %% @step The browser is told to wait a second and try again with the same key.
            %% @payload.headers Retry-After: 1
            %% @payload.body { "error": "request_in_progress", "message": "A payment with this key is already being processed." }
            %% @ref src/app.ts:41
            APP-->>FE: 409 Conflict<br/>Retry-After: 1
        else Different body
            %% @step The key was reused for a different amount or cart. That is a client bug, so it is refused rather than guessed at.
            %% @ref src/middleware/idempotency.middleware.ts:41
            MW-->>APP: next(new UnprocessableError("idempotency_key_mismatch"))
            %% @step The browser gets a 422 explaining the key does not match the body.
            %% @payload.body { "error": "idempotency_key_mismatch", "message": "This key was used with a different request body." }
            %% @ref src/app.ts:41
            APP-->>FE: 422 Unprocessable Entity<br/>"This key was used with a different request body."
        end
    end

    %% @section Charge once
    %% @summary The owner of the key goes through Controller, Service and Repository and charges the card exactly once.
    %% @step This request owns the key, so the middleware chain ends and the controller takes over.
    %% @ref src/middleware/idempotency.middleware.ts:45
    MW->>CTRL: next() → PaymentController.create()
    %% @step The controller passes the user and the validated body to the service. It holds no business rules itself.
    %% @payload.input { "userId": "u_9", "cartId": "cart_77", "amount": 149900, "currency": "INR", "idempotencyKey": "4f9c…" }
    %% @ref src/controllers/payment.controller.ts:14
    CTRL->>SVC: PaymentService.createPayment(u_9, dto)
    %% @step The payment is saved as pending first, so there is a record even if the provider call fails.
    %% @payload.input { "userId": "u_9", "cartId": "cart_77", "amount": 149900, "currency": "INR", "status": "pending" }
    %% @ref src/services/payment.service.ts:22
    SVC->>REPO: PaymentRepository.create({ status: "pending" })
    %% @step The repository writes the row and asks Postgres for the new id.
    %% @payload.sql INSERT INTO payments (user_id, cart_id, amount, currency, status) VALUES ('u_9', 'cart_77', 149900, 'INR', 'pending') RETURNING id
    %% @state before no payment for cart_77
    %% @state after payment pay_456 with status pending
    %% @ref src/repositories/payment.repository.ts:11
    REPO->>DB: INSERT INTO payments … RETURNING id
    %% @step Postgres returns the id of the new row.
    DB-->>REPO: 1 row: pay_456
    %% @step The service gets the new payment back, still pending.
    %% @payload.result { "id": "pay_456", "status": "pending", "amount": 149900 }
    %% @ref src/repositories/payment.repository.ts:15
    REPO-->>SVC: payment pay_456 (pending)
    %% @step The key is forwarded to the provider too, so even a retry between us and the provider cannot charge twice.
    %% @payload.headers Idempotency-Key: 4f9c2e1a-7b7d-4c55-9a51-3e0d6b2f8c10
    %% @payload.body { "amount": 149900, "currency": "inr", "source": "tok_visa", "metadata": { "payment_id": "pay_456" } }
    %% @retry 2 retries on a network error, same key each time, so a retry can never double charge.
    %% @uses lib psp-sdk src/lib/psp.ts:4 The provider's client library; it adds the auth header and retries on network errors.
    %% @ref src/services/payment.service.ts:30
    SVC->>PSP: POST https://api.psp.example/v1/charges<br/>Idempotency-Key: 4f9c…
    %% @step The provider confirms the charge and returns its id.
    %% @payload.body { "id": "ch_789", "status": "succeeded", "amount": 149900 }
    PSP-->>SVC: 200 OK<br/>ch_789, status: succeeded
    %% @step The service records the charge id and marks the payment as paid.
    %% @payload.input { "id": "pay_456", "chargeId": "ch_789" }
    %% @ref src/services/payment.service.ts:38
    SVC->>REPO: PaymentRepository.markPaid(pay_456, ch_789)
    %% @step Only a pending payment can become paid, so a stray second update changes nothing.
    %% @payload.sql UPDATE payments SET status = 'paid', charge_id = 'ch_789' WHERE id = 'pay_456' AND status = 'pending'
    %% @state before pay_456 pending, no charge_id
    %% @state after pay_456 paid with charge_id ch_789
    %% @ref src/repositories/payment.repository.ts:24
    REPO->>DB: UPDATE payments SET status = 'paid'<br/>WHERE id = 'pay_456' AND status = 'pending'
    %% @step One row changed: the payment is paid.
    DB-->>REPO: 1 row updated
    %% @step The service gets the paid payment back.
    %% @payload.result { "id": "pay_456", "status": "paid", "chargeId": "ch_789" }
    %% @ref src/repositories/payment.repository.ts:28
    REPO-->>SVC: payment pay_456 (paid)
    %% @step The service announces the payment and moves on. It does not wait for, or know about, the receipt email.
    %% @payload.event { "type": "payment.paid", "paymentId": "pay_456", "userId": "u_9", "email": "ana@example.com", "amount": 149900 }
    %% @async fire-and-forget; the listener runs after the current call stack.
    %% @ref src/services/payment.service.ts:41
    SVC-)EB: emit("payment.paid")
    %% @step The controller gets the paid payment back from the service.
    %% @payload.result { "id": "pay_456", "status": "paid", "amount": 149900, "currency": "INR", "chargeId": "ch_789", "userId": "u_9" }
    %% @ref src/services/payment.service.ts:43
    SVC-->>CTRL: payment pay_456 (paid)
    %% @step The serializer keeps only the fields the client may see; the charge id and user id stay on the server.
    %% @ref src/controllers/payment.controller.ts:15
    CTRL->>SER: PaymentSerializer.toJSON(payment)
    %% @step The response body comes back to the controller.
    %% @payload.result { "id": "pay_456", "status": "paid", "amount": 149900, "currency": "INR" }
    %% @field id pay_456, created by the INSERT into payments in this request
    %% @ref src/serializers/payment.serializer.ts:6
    SER-->>CTRL: { id, status, amount, currency }
    %% @step The controller sends 201. The idempotency middleware wrapped res.json earlier, so the call lands in its wrapper first.
    %% @payload.body { "id": "pay_456", "status": "paid", "amount": 149900, "currency": "INR" }
    %% @ref src/controllers/payment.controller.ts:16
    CTRL-->>MW: res.status(201).json(body)
    %% @step The response is saved against the key, so any later repeat gets the same answer without charging again.
    %% @payload.input SET idem:4f9c… '{"status":"done","code":201,"body":{"id":"pay_456",…}}' XX KEEPTTL
    %% @ref src/middleware/idempotency.middleware.ts:52
    MW->>RDS: SET idem:4f9c… status done, code 201, body<br/>XX KEEPTTL
    %% @step Redis confirms the saved answer.
    RDS-->>MW: OK
    %% @step The wrapper hands the body to the real res.json, which the app sends.
    %% @effect Log line "payment created" with payment_id and key, for support searches
    %% @ref src/middleware/idempotency.middleware.ts:55
    MW-->>APP: res.json(body)
    %% @step The browser gets the result of the one and only charge.
    %% @payload.body { "id": "pay_456", "status": "paid", "amount": 149900, "currency": "INR" }
    %% @ref src/controllers/payment.controller.ts:16
    APP-->>FE: 201 Created<br/>pay_456, status: paid

    %% @section Finish
    %% @summary The key is thrown away once the money has moved, so the next purchase starts fresh.
    %% @step The payment is settled, so the key is removed. The next checkout starts with a fresh key.
    %% @uses hook useIdempotencyKey src/checkout/useIdempotencyKey.ts:21 Its clear() removes the key for this cart from sessionStorage.
    %% @ref src/checkout/pay.ts:57
    FE->>FE: Clear key from sessionStorage
    %% @step The user sees one receipt for one charge, however many times they tapped.
    %% @ref src/checkout/pay.ts:59
    FE-->>U: Receipt for ₹1,499

    %% @section Receipt email
    %% @summary After the reply, the event bus, the mail queue and the mail worker send the receipt without slowing the payment down.
    %% @step The payment.paid listener puts a receipt job on the mail queue and returns at once.
    %% @payload.event { "name": "payment-receipt", "data": { "paymentId": "pay_456", "email": "ana@example.com", "amount": 149900 } }
    %% @retry the job is tried 3 times with exponential backoff from 5 s
    %% @async runs after the response has been sent
    %% @ref src/events/payment.events.ts:9
    EB->>MQ: mailQueue.add("payment-receipt")
    %% @step The mail worker process takes the next job from the queue.
    %% @payload.event { "id": "71", "name": "payment-receipt", "data": { "paymentId": "pay_456", "email": "ana@example.com", "amount": 149900 } }
    MQ->>MWK: job payment-receipt (pay_456)
    %% @step The worker renders the receipt and hands it to the email provider.
    %% @payload.body { "to": "ana@example.com", "template": "receipt", "data": { "payment_id": "pay_456", "amount": "₹1,499" } }
    %% @uses util renderTemplate src/utils/template.ts:3 Fills the receipt template with the payment data.
    %% @ref src/workers/mail.worker.ts:18
    MWK->>MAIL: POST https://api.mail.example/v3/send
    %% @step The provider accepts the email and returns its id; the job is marked done, so it is not retried.
    %% @payload.body { "id": "msg_1", "status": "queued" }
    MAIL-->>MWK: 202 Accepted<br/>msg_1
